 Runnable processes: each application tile runs as `app-<id>` from `src/processes/<id>.ts`, healthy once its ready marker exists; HTTP processes answer `GET /healthz` on port 3000 inside the Compose network. `app-edge` publishes each public HTTP process at `http://127.0.0.1:$LOCAL_HTTP_PORT/api/<id>/` and answers `GET /healthz` itself; internal ones are reached only inside the network at `http://app-<id>:3000`. A module's routes are under `/modules/<module id>`: a stub or a generated hook in `src/modules/` answers 501 `NOT_IMPLEMENTED` until you write it. A consumer module or worker template runs a loop on its queue (a topic's `<topic>--<id>` subscriber): "Not implemented" sends the message to `<queue>.failed` with one log line, other errors retry within the loop's limit (a stub's 5 times, waiting 250 ms doubling up to 10 s), an unreachable store waits 5 s and keeps the attempt, a failed `fetch` to an outside host keeps the normal retries, and `pnpm stop` drains the loops. Every store or channel a stub links to is provisioned for its tile with its links' widest access (a database login with CONNECT and USAGE on public, SELECT on the database's `published` views and on another owner's template views; a queue, topic or subscriber; a cache; a bucket key; a search service with no index), and `src/runtime/module-clients.ts` gives each stub real clients, each probed once at startup. Each module that writes a database has its own SQL file, `infra/postgres/<database>/modules/<module>.sql`, yours to write: `first-run` and `start` run it every time, after the template migrations and views, as the setup-only owner in schema `m_<module>`, so keep it re-runnable (`IF NOT EXISTS`, `CREATE OR REPLACE`). Only its tile's login uses that schema; every login on the database may SELECT what it publishes as views in `published`, and nothing else of its tables. `first-run` and `start` start them only while `processes.runnable` in .jeeves/report.json is true; its `processes.tiles` lists each process's routes, loops and provisioned stores. No restart policy: a failed process stays exited until `pnpm start`.

-**Endpoints from the environment.** Each process reads the endpoints it uses from its environment as it builds each client, with no default: `JEEVES_DATABASE_<ID>_HOST` (a host, or an absolute Unix socket directory) and `_PORT` for each database, `JEEVES_CACHE_<ID>_HOST` and `_PORT` for each cache, `JEEVES_BROKER_HOST` and `JEEVES_BROKER_PORT` for the broker, and `JEEVES_TILE_<ID>_URL` (absolute `http:` or `https:`, with no credentials, query or fragment) for each tile its stubs call, where `<ID>` is the tile ID upper-cased with `-` as `_`. A process that publishes to or consumes a Pub/Sub topic reads `JEEVES_PUBSUB_PROJECT`, a Google Cloud project ID (a lowercase letter, then up to 29 lowercase letters, digits or `-`, optionally after `<domain>:`); `compose.yaml` also sets `PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST` for the local emulator and `METADATA_SERVER_DETECTION: none`, so the client looks for no Google metadata server; a deployment leaves both unset. Each External API its stubs call is at `JEEVES_EXTERNAL_<ID>_URL`, by the same URL rule, which `compose.yaml` doesn't set: the value is yours, so set it in `compose.override.yaml` (and in your deployment); until it is set the process fails startup with `Invalid endpoint JEEVES_EXTERNAL_<ID>_URL`. Here `app-billing-api` reads `JEEVES_EXTERNAL_STRIPE_API_URL`; `app-link-api` reads `JEEVES_EXTERNAL_REPUTATION_API_URL`. A failed call to an External API keeps a loop's normal retries. A process whose stubs call the Identity reads `JEEVES_IDENTITY_<ID>_URL`, the Identity's base URL as the process reaches it, by the same URL rule: `compose.yaml` sets Keycloak's address inside the network, `http://identity-<id>:8080` (a stub adds the realm's paths, `/realms/<id>/...`); a deployment sets its issuer's origin, or another address its processes reach. A sign-in's `OIDC_ISSUER` is what tokens carry, which a container can't reach locally, so it isn't the client's base. A lost connection to the Identity waits as an unreachable store's does. A missing or invalid one fails startup with `Invalid endpoint <variable>`, never its value. Each database and cache also reads its transport, `JEEVES_DATABASE_<ID>_TLS` or `JEEVES_CACHE_<ID>_TLS`, with no default: `disable` (plain text, which `PGSSLMODE` can't change) or `verify-full` (TLS that checks the certificate chain and the host name); with `verify-full`, `_CA_FILE` is an optional PEM file (else Node's roots; one that can't be read fails startup with `Unreadable CA file <variable>`) and `_TLS_SERVERNAME` the optional name the certificate carries when it isn't the host (a managed instance reached by a private address). With `JEEVES_REQUIRE_TLS=true` (else unset), startup fails with `Insecure endpoint <variable>` for a database or cache whose transport isn't `verify-full` (a database's Unix socket directory is local transport and passes), a `JEEVES_TILE_<ID>_URL`, `JEEVES_EXTERNAL_<ID>_URL`, `JEEVES_IDENTITY_<ID>_URL`, `OIDC_ISSUER` or `OIDC_JWKS_URL` that isn't `https:`, and any `PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST`; the broker, object storage and search are plain text and aren't checked. `compose.yaml` sets the local values (`disable`, and no `JEEVES_REQUIRE_TLS`); a deployment sets its own. Object storage and search are still reached at their local names. Each process reads (also `processes.tiles[].environment` in .jeeves/report.json):
